1
00:04:13,043 --> 00:04:19,043
The fastest hands in the East meet the biggest mouth in the West.

2
00:08:26,086 --> 00:08:32,086
Rush Hour is a action, comedy, crime film directed by Brett Ratner.

3
00:12:39,130 --> 00:12:45,130
When Hong Kong Inspector Lee is summoned to Los Angeles to investigate a kidnapping, the FBI doesn't want
any outside help and assigns cocky LAPD Detective James Carter to distract Lee from the case.

4
00:16:52,173 --> 00:16:58,173
Not content to watch the action from the sidelines, Lee and Carter form an
unlikely partnership and investigate the case themselves.

5
00:21:05,217 --> 00:21:11,217
Lee, played by Jackie Chan.

6
00:25:18,260 --> 00:25:24,260
Carter, played by Chris Tucker.

7
00:29:31,304 --> 00:29:37,304
Griffin, played by Tom Wilkinson.

8
00:33:44,347 --> 00:33:50,347
Captain Diel, played by Philip Baker Hall.

9
00:37:57,391 --> 00:38:03,391
Johnson, played by Elizabeth Peña.

10
00:42:10,434 --> 00:42:16,434
Clive, played by Chris Penn.

11
00:46:23,478 --> 00:46:29,478
Rush Hour is a 1998 American buddy cop action comedy film directed by Brett Ratner
from a screenplay by Jim Kouf and Ross LaManna and a story by LaManna.

12
00:50:36,521 --> 00:50:42,521
It stars Jackie Chan, Chris Tucker, Tom Wilkinson,
Chris Penn, and Elizabeth Peña.

13
00:54:49,565 --> 00:54:55,565
In the film, Hong Kong Police Force (HKPF) Chief Inspector Lee (Chan) and Los Angeles Police Department (LAPD)
Detective James Carter (Tucker) are forced to work together to rescue a Chinese diplomat's abducted daughter.

14
00:59:02,608 --> 00:59:08,608
Development on Rush Hour began in 1995, after LaManna wrote a spec script, which was first
initially sold to Hollywood Pictures with Ratner and Chan both attached to the project.

15
01:03:15,652 --> 01:03:21,652
The film was eventually shelved until being acquired by New Line Cinema, who had an
established relationship with Ratner and Tucker following Money Talks (1997).

16
01:07:28,695 --> 01:07:34,695
Several actors were considered prior to Tucker signing onto the
project, with the rest of the cast rounded out by early 1997.

17
01:11:41,739 --> 01:11:47,739
Principal photography began that November and lasted until January 1998, with
filming locations including California, Los Angeles and Hong Kong.

18
01:15:54,782 --> 01:16:00,782
Rush Hour was theatrically released in the United States
on September 18, 1998, by New Line Cinema.

19
01:20:07,826 --> 01:20:13,826
The film received mixed-to-positive reviews from critics, with
praise for Tucker and Chan's chemistry and performances.

20
01:24:20,869 --> 01:24:26,869
It also grossed $245.3 million worldwide and was followed by two
sequels: Rush Hour 2 (2001) and Rush Hour 3 (2007).

21
01:28:33,913 --> 01:28:39,913
The film explores themes of martial arts, fbi, two man army, chinese mafia, cop.

22
01:32:46,956 --> 01:32:52,956
The tone is lighthearted.
